From: h0cheung <hcheunh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm: clamp the synthetic EDID pixel clock to the DTD limit

The synthetic EDID generator bails out when the preferred mode's pixel clock
exceeds 655.35 MHz, because an EDID detailed timing stores it in a 16-bit
field of 10 kHz units. The Y700's panel is 1904x3040 and its 120 Hz mode runs
at 915.6 MHz, so every mode is rejected, the connector keeps no EDID, and
ParseEDID returns before it ever fills ValidDynamicRefreshRates - Steam then
sees a single refresh rate and hides the selector entirely.

The detailed timing only describes the panel to EDID readers; the mode itself
comes from the kernel and the refresh list is collected from the connector's
kernel modes. Cap the clock instead of dropping the whole EDID.
---
 src/edid.cpp | 11 +++++++++--
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/edid.cpp b/src/edid.cpp
index 6c835ce..740bfd7 100644
--- a/src/edid.cpp
+++ b/src/edid.cpp
@@ -363,7 +363,7 @@ namespace gamescope
 
     std::vector<uint8_t> GenerateSimpleEdid( const EdidModeInfo &mode )
     {
-        if ( !mode.clockKHz || mode.clockKHz > 655350 ||
+        if ( !mode.clockKHz ||
              !mode.hdisplay || mode.hdisplay > 4095 ||
              mode.hsyncStart < mode.hdisplay || mode.hsyncEnd < mode.hsyncStart || mode.htotal < mode.hsyncEnd ||
              !mode.vdisplay || mode.vdisplay > 4095 ||
@@ -391,8 +391,15 @@ namespace gamescope
         const uint32_t vsyncOffset = std::min<uint32_t>( mode.vsyncStart - mode.vdisplay, 63 );
         const uint32_t vsyncWidth = std::clamp<uint32_t>( mode.vsyncEnd - mode.vsyncStart, 1, 63 );
 
-        const uint32_t pixelClock10KHz = ( mode.clockKHz + 5 ) / 10;
-        if ( !pixelClock10KHz || pixelClock10KHz > UINT16_MAX )
+        // The DTD pixel-clock field is 16-bit in 10 kHz units, so a timing above
+        // 655.35 MHz cannot be encoded. A 3K panel at a high refresh needs more
+        // (the Y700's 1904x3040@120 is 915.6 MHz), and rejecting it here would
+        // drop the whole synthetic EDID and, with it, every refresh rate the
+        // connector advertises. The detailed timing only describes the panel to
+        // EDID readers - the mode itself comes from the kernel - so cap the
+        // clock instead of failing the mode.
+        const uint32_t pixelClock10KHz = std::min<uint32_t>( ( mode.clockKHz + 5 ) / 10, UINT16_MAX );
+        if ( !pixelClock10KHz )
             return {};
 
         std::vector<uint8_t> edid = GenerateSimpleEdid( mode.hdisplay, mode.vdisplay );
